summary: System reboot verification on loading a corrupted initramfs
description: |
    The purpose of this test is to verify that the system successfully reboots when
    it attempts to load a deliberately corrupted initramfs image during the boot
    sequence. This demonstrates proper error detection and recovery mechanisms in
    the event of initramfs corruption, preventing a stalled or unbootable system.
    Test Inputs:
        corrupt initramfs: dd if=/dev/zero of=/boot/initramfs-$(uname -r).img bs=1 count=100 conv=notrunc
    Expected results:
        [   PASS   ] :: Command 'dd if=/dev/zero of=/boot/initramfs-5.14.0-570.518.16.1.16.el9_6iv.aarch64.img bs=1 count=100 conv=notrunc' (Expected 0, got 0)
        [   PASS   ] :: Command 'aboot-update -i /boot/initramfs-5.14.0-570.518.16.1.16.el9_6iv.aarch64.img $(uname -r)' (Expected 0, got 0)
        [   PASS   ] :: Command 'aboot-deploy -l aboot-5.14.0-570.518.16.1.16.el9_6iv.aarch64.img' (Expected 0, got 0)
        SUT keeps rebooting and test timeouts. Message expected on console:
        - Kernel panic - not syncing: VFS: Unable to mount root fs on unknown-block(0,0)
    Results location:
        output.txt
        console.log
contact: Pablo Ridolfi <pridolfi@redhat.com>
id: 571c06c7-4683-4b71-8f30-13ce1fc37092
component:
  - kernel
test: bash ./runtest.sh initramfs
framework: beakerlib
restart-on-exit-code:
    - 255
restart-max-count: 2
duration: 5m
adjust+:
    enabled: false
    when: hw_target != rcar_s4 and hw_target != ridesx4
